    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Parc naturel départemental du Vinaigrier covers 31 hectares on the heights of Nice, east of the city and on the edge of Villefranche-sur-Mer. Mont Vinaigrier is a limestone plateau about 370 metres high, planted with tall Aleppo pines and holm oaks, with slopes of varying steepness and, on the gentler ones, old farming terraces ("restanques") planted with olive trees. The park offers one of the finest panoramas over the Baie des Anges and the Villefranche roadstead, as far as the Estérel massif (Département, tourist office). Owned by the Conservatoire du littoral, it is managed by the Alpes-Maritimes Département; entry is free (Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The park's regulation, signed on 17 December 2019 by the President of the Alpes-Maritimes Département, requires dogs to wear a collar and be kept on a leash all year round: straying is forbidden. Category 1 dogs are banned and category 2 dogs must be muzzled. Animals are also banned from the landscaped areas (play areas, educational areas, fitness areas, sports circuits), for safety and hygiene reasons. Animals must not disturb other visitors or wildlife: the Département lists hedgehogs, squirrels, foxes, badgers and 59 bird species in the park.</p>
      <p>The park is open from 1 April to 31 October from 7:30am to 8pm, and from 1 November to 31 March from 8am to 6pm (regulation and Département page); these hours may be changed for service or safety reasons, with a notice at the entrance. The discovery route proposed by the Département takes 1h15 and is rated medium difficulty. The Département equips the site with an observation area, a viewpoint table and a themed trail, and asks visitors to bring excellent hiking shoes, as you must stay very alert to natural obstacles and the weather. Some sections of the trail are closed to the public: follow the signs.</p>
      <p>On the practical side, the park has a 50-space car park at 315 boulevard de l'Observatoire. From Nice, take the Grande Corniche: the upper entrance is on the right, about 50 metres after the observatory. By bus, line 84 stops at "Observatoire", then it is a 300-metre walk via avenue Brès; the CRT Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur mentions the "Sainte-Anne" stop. For shade, the CRT notes pleasant coolness in some parts of the park in summer, especially in the morning or late afternoon, under the Aleppo pines and holm oaks. None of the sources read mentions a water point: bring water for your dog.</p>
      <p><strong>Worth checking before you go:</strong> the Département's "Explorateurs des Alpes-Maritimes" page gives opening hours of 7am to 8pm from April to October, while the park page and the regulation give 7:30am: if in doubt, rely on the notice at the entrance.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: a good option for a short, shaded walk with your dog without leaving Nice, especially in the late afternoon once the heat eases off.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The park overlooks the Villefranche-sur-Mer roadstead and the Baie des Anges, which you can see from the observation area and the viewpoint table.</p>
